# Older C33 cores divide with a multi-step div0s/div1/div2s sequence, but
# the PE manual explicitly removes all five divide-step instructions.  So
# there is no divmodsi4 pattern and GCC calls libgcc for integer division.
# libgcc2.c only supplies the double-word routines
# (__divdi3 and friends), so the single-word ones have to come from here.
#
# The sign handling and the two wrappers are GCC's own generic C, shipped in
# this tree.  The original toolchain reached the same conclusion: patch 0003
# in host-tools/toolchain-patches replaced EPSON's hand-written divide-step
# assembly with these files because those instructions do not exist on PE.
# They live in libgcc/ now; in gcc 3.3 they were under libgcc/config/.
#
#   config/c33/udivmodsi4.c  __udivmodsi4   the actual long division
#   udivmod.c                __udivsi3, __umodsi3
#   divmod.c                 __divsi3,  __modsi3   (sign handling around it)
#
# The long division itself is ours: the generic one produces all 32 quotient
# bits whatever the operands are, which costs about 1,600 cycles a divide on
# this core.  See config/c33/udivmodsi4.c.

LIB2ADD += \
	$(srcdir)/config/c33/udivmodsi4.c \
	$(srcdir)/udivmod.c \
	$(srcdir)/divmod.c

# The four entry points are jumps into __udivmodsi4, and on this machine an
# application may want that one routine somewhere the ordinary call cannot
# reach: internal RAM is at the bottom of the map and the program runs from
# SDRAM at 0x10000000, 268 MB away, which is why a divide placed in A0 RAM
# used to need a hand-written bridge.  Long calls here cost one instruction
# word in each wrapper and let a linker script put the division wherever the
# fetch is cheapest, with no second copy of it.
udivmod.o udivmod_s.o divmod.o divmod_s.o: LIBGCC2_CFLAGS += -mlong-calls
